{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And he took from what came to his hand, etc.</b> Above I explained that this is stated according to what he showed Esau outwardly, for all this is neither from heaven nor from earth, but only from what came to his hand through his labor and strength and the might of his arm. But according to the truth, Jacob acknowledged that HaShem is the one who gives him strength to prosper, and this little that came to his hand through his labor would burst forth into abundance, and the blessing of HaShem is what enriches. And it was grievous in Jacob's eyes to give of the blessing of HaShem to this wicked man, for this superabundance flows from a holy place and this wicked man is not worthy to partake of it. But because there was also mingled within it this little that came to his hand by way of nature and the wages of his labor, from that portion Jacob considered it permissible to take and send a gift to the wicked man — therefore it says 'from what came to his hand.'" "And some say: 'from what came to his hand' specifically — the animals that are in his hand and under his authority, these he sent him as a gift, but not the shepherds, because they had converted and been freed and are no longer in Jacob's hand. Therefore it says, 'For Esau will ask you, To whom do you belong and where are you going, and whose are these before you?' (Genesis 32:18) — for perhaps he will think that I am sending him a gift of the animals together with the shepherds. And you shall say, 'To your servant' (Genesis 32:19) — and this is an answer to the question 'To whom do you belong': to tell that the shepherds are not his, only the gift is sent to my lord."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:31:35.772308206Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}